  "worked_solution": "First, we note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(-6{x}^{7}) & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(-6\\cdot {x}^{7}) \\\\  & =-6\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph $y={x}^{7}$ is a power function with an odd positive power and therefore has the property that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{7}=-\\infty .\n$$\n\nMultiplication by the negative constant $-6$ changes the sign of the limit. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{7} & =-{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{7}=\\infty . & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(-6{x}^{7})=\\infty .\n$$",
